{"author":"Rebar Bot","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"## ROOT CAUSE PROVEN (Phase 1 complete) — the async gate surface loses the op-cert signer binding\n\n### The mechanism\n\n`review_plan_start` / `verify_completion_start` run the gate on a bare\n`threading.Thread(target=_bg, daemon=True)` (`src/rebar/_mcp_llm.py:194`, `_spawn_gate_daemon`).\nA new `threading.Thread` starts with a **fresh, empty `contextvars.Context`**, so it does NOT\ninherit `_BOUND` — the `ContextVar` that `bound_signer()` sets in the uvicorn serving thread\n(`src/rebar/_mcp_serving.py:157`).\n\nInside that thread the two halves of the signing identity resolve from **independent** sources\n(`src/rebar/_opcert_signing.py`):\n\n- `opcert_principal()` (line 212) → binding is `None` → falls to `REBAR_OPCERT_ENV_ID`, a\n  **process-global env var that every thread shares** → still the pinned\n  `9f1c8e42-7a3b-4d5e-b6c1-2f0a9d8e7c65` (`nava-opcert-prod`).\n- `ensure_opcert_key()` (line 155) → binding is `None`, and the mcp compose service sets\n  **`REBAR_IDENTITY_SIGNING_KEY`, not `REBAR_OPCERT_KEY_PATH`** (`infra/compose/docker-compose.yml:467`\n  vs the opcert service's `:357`) → falls through to the `<tracker>/.opcert-key` genesis, which\n  **silently auto-generates a fresh Ed25519 key** and signs with it.\n\nResult: a structurally valid cert that **claims a pinned principal but is signed by an untrusted\nkey**. On verify, `_opcert_trust_root` (`_opcert_verify.py:130`) takes the `pinned_environment`\nbranch, `ssh-keygen -Y verify` runs against the pinned public key, and exits **255** →\n`mismatch` → `no certified plan-review attestation`. Exactly the reported `--status` output.\n\nThe synchronous `review_plan` path uses `anyio.to_thread.run_sync` (`_mcp_llm.py:291,344`), which\n**does** propagate the caller's context — which is why sync reviews certify and async ones do not.\n\n`compose_startup_opcert_binding` already documents this precise failure as the thing the binding\nexists to prevent (`src/rebar/mcp_server.py:705-708`): *\"WITHOUT it the certified tools would sign\nthe principal from REBAR_OPCERT_ENV_ID but under the wrong (genesis) key, so the cert would fail\nthe required-environment verify.\"* And `_mcp_serving.py:126-129` already names the hazard: *\"a\ncontextvars.ContextVar set on the main thread is NOT inherited by the background thread.\"* The\nasync gate daemon repeats the mistake the serving thread had already fixed.\n\n### Discriminating experiment (runtime, not static)\n\nBound a signer, then resolved the signing identity from three execution contexts:\n\n| path | binding visible | signs under |\n|---|---|---|\n| serving thread, inline | **True** | deployed/pinned key |\n| `anyio.to_thread.run_sync` (**sync** `review_plan`) | **True** | deployed/pinned key |\n| `threading.Thread(daemon=True)` (**async** `*_start`) | **False** | **auto-generated genesis key** |\n\nPrincipal was identical (`9f1c8e42-…`) in all three. Two distinct signing keys under one claimed\nprincipal — the divergence, reproduced.\n\n### Store evidence (independent of the reproduction)\n\nThe original bad SIGNATURE event survives as a retired file\n(`.tickets-tracker/e956-b1c3-45b9-4016/1788561835000337312-…-SIGNATURE.json.retired`):\n`principal: 9f1c8e42-…` but `env_id: eea68b7f-…`, and the SSHSIG blob's embedded public key is\n`ssh-ed25519 …INlyKV/nkmannDmyqRKaC5cvOJ3eFIon4qd3Uc0M1KBc` — **not** the key pinned for that\nenv_id in `.rebar/trusted_environments.yaml`\n(`…IB6u+ddQiB4ck/ToORQkQwtehODF4VqLirMIUNFpobZZ`). The recovery signature has\n`principal == env_id == b8a354e0-…` and the local box's own key.\n\n### AC2 — the audit\n\nSwept every SIGNATURE event in the store (8,620 tickets): **4,797 sshsig attestations** (plus 1,479\nlegacy HMAC).\n\n- **82 fail verification (1.7%)**.\n- **81 of the 82** are principal `9f1c8e42-…` signed by the unpinned genesis key\n  `…OJ3eFIon4qd3Uc0M1KBc`, first **2026-08-31 22:22**, last **2026-09-05 00:08**.\n- The **same** environment (`store_env eea68b7f-…`) also produced **60 certs under the correct\n  pinned key** over an **overlapping** window (2026-08-27 → 2026-09-04). Two live keys, one claimed\n  principal, interleaved — the sync/async split, not a key rotation.\n- The remaining 1 failure is an unrelated one-off (`b8a354e0`, 2026-08-23).\n\nSo: **not rare, and not routine either** — it is systematic for one surface (async MCP gate starts)\nand clean for every other. Severity is real: every async-started gate since 2026-08-31 has minted an\nattestation that cannot be certified off the signing box.\n\n### Why nothing fired at sign time (AC3/AC4)\n\n`_warn_if_verify_cannot_resolve` (`_opcert_signing.py:294`) exists for exactly this class (bug\n879b) but is **blind here**: it asks whether the *signing process* can resolve the signing key's\npublic half. Inside the unbound daemon thread `_opcert_own_key_paths` also has no binding, so it\nresolves the *same* genesis key, the check passes, and the guard stays silent. It validates\nself-consistency; it never validates **principal ↔ key** consistency against the pinned trust root\n— which is the only check that would have caught this.\n\n### Defect attribution — `caused_by` d80d-7be7-1c0a-4231\n\n- Obligation created **2026-08-24** by `5083d3d36f92` (\"Sign MCP-minted op-certs under the box env\"):\n  certified ops must execute under the bound signer.\n- Obligation unmet by **`44ec1c2e6737`** (2026-08-30), *\"d80d-7be7-1c0a-4231: async gate start (P2)\"*,\n  which added `_spawn_gate_daemon` — a **new execution context for certified ops** — without\n  carrying the binding into it.\n- Date test agrees: the first genesis-key cert is **2026-08-31 22:22**, one day after that commit;\n  every attestation before it verifies.\n\nThis is an **unmet obligation**, not a regression in the code being fixed. Note the divergence from\na naive blame: blaming the *failing* site would name `_opcert_signing.py`/`_opcert_verify.py`\n(untouched and correct), or the compose file. The reviewable escape is d80d — a reviewer of \"move\ngate execution onto a background thread\" could plausibly have asked what context that thread loses.\n\n### Legitimacy: `defect`\n\nViolates a cited design invariant (`mcp_server.py:705-708`) and the pinned trust root in\n`.rebar/trusted_environments.yaml`. Not config: the compose file's `REBAR_IDENTITY_SIGNING_KEY` is\nconsumed correctly by `compose_startup_opcert_binding`; the binding is composed, it is simply lost\nat the thread boundary.\n\n### Parity sibling\n\n`run_workflow`'s daemon (`src/rebar/_mcp_writes.py:603`) is the same construct with the same\ncontext loss — the async gate starter was explicitly written to \"mirror `run_workflow`\". Disposition\nin Phase 2.\n\nPhase 2 (RED→GREEN) proceeding."},"env_id":"eea68b7f-6697-488b-ac52-744e2ed8e96f","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1788569760828313821,"uuid":"e7ba6e6d-23f2-42bb-9570-76037a1a57e0"}